{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'nl -ba /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1792289000000-architect-antlr-multi-plan.claude-cloud-fable.findings.md'","aggregated_output":"     1\t# ANTLR multi-target plan: one grammar, one parse-tree IR, N hosts, any language\n     2\t\n     3\tArchitect plan answering `collab/1792289000000-architect-antlr-multi-plan.prompt.md`.\n     4\tThis plan gates the engineer round `collab/1792282000000-engineer-antlr-multi-target.prompt.md`\n     5\t(no worktree `datomworld-antlr-multi` exists yet; nothing of that round has started).\n     6\t\n     7\t## Context\n     8\t\n     9\t`(lang :yang.python/antlr)` works on the JVM only. The generator\n    10\t(`src/dev/yang_antlr_gen.clj`, `bb gen:python-antlr`) produces the Java target\n    11\tonly, `src/clj/yang/antlr/cst.clj` is the only parse-tree exporter, and\n    12\t`src/cljc/yin/repl/frontends.cljc` installs the ANTLR frontend under\n    13\t`#?(:cljd nil :clj ...)`. Node and Dart answer `unavailable-parser`.\n    14\t\n    15\tOwner rulings (verbatim): \"don't remove legacy until antlr works for JS and dart\n    16\ttoo\"; \"future languages will be added too and whatever is design should make it\n    17\teasy to add new antlr supported languages\"; \"maybe an antlr intermediate\n    18\trepresentation is needed if antlr doesn't have that concept already.\"\n    19\t\n    20\tFacts established during exploration (all verified against the pinned sources):\n    21\t\n    22\t- grammars-v4 `python/python3` at the pinned revision `8af0d4c2` ships helper\n    23\t  bases for **Java, JavaScript, Dart**, C#, C++, Go, Python3, TypeScript. Its\n    24\t  `desc.xml` lists tested targets `Cpp;CSharp;Java;JavaScript;Python3`: the\n    25\t  **Dart helper is not exercised by upstream CI**.\n    26\t- The JS and Dart lexer bases carry the same NEWLINE/DEDENT `commonToken`\n    27\t  construction the Java base has (the one the manifest's `:patches` fixes so\n    28\t  every token's span is its source). The patch must be ported per target.\n    29\t- JS helpers: `import antlr4 from 'antlr4'` (ESM), `Python3LexerBase extends\n    30\t  antlr4.Lexer`, imports `./Python3Parser.js` (circular with the generated\n    31\t  lexer; upstream ships it that way). Dart helpers: `import\n    32\t  'package:antlr4/antlr4.dart'`, `abstract class Python3LexerBase extends\n    33\t  Lexer`, plus `dart:io`. Neither JS nor Dart helper has a licence notice, the\n    34\t  same unresolved situation as the two Java helpers.\n    35\t- Runtimes: npm `antlr4` 4.13.2 (BSD-3-Clause, ESM, `engines node>=16`, no\n    36\t  runtime deps); pub `antlr4` 4.13.2 (BSD-3-Clause, SDK `>=2.12.0 <3.0.0`,\n    37\t  deps `logging`, `collection`). Both equal the pinned tool 4.13.2.\n    38\t- ANTLR has **no target-neutral tree representation**: each target materializes\n    39\t  its own `ParserRuleContext`/`TerminalNode` objects. The repo already has the\n    40\t  neutral form: the CST packet v1 documented in `cst.clj` lines 16-35 and read\n    41\t  by `src/cljc/yang/antlr/packet.cljc`. The lowering\n    42\t  (`src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/lower.cljc`) reads only `:kind :rule :children\n    43\t  :type :text :span :synthetic` through `packet/*`. Hand-built packets already\n    44\t  drive the lowering on Node and Dart (`lower_portable_test.cljc`, the\n    45\t  precompiled `packets` of `test/yin/repl/python_antlr_test.cljc`, whose\n    46\t  `precompiled-packets-are-the-parsers-test` asserts they equal the JVM\n    47\t  parser's output).\n    48\t- The TS design (`yang.typescript.md` 5.3) and PHP design (`yang.php.md` 6)\n    49\t  both say \"the local parser is JVM-only; Node and Dart go through packets or a\n    50\t  configured service\" for a **concrete** reason: upstream TypeScript helpers\n    51\t  exist for Java/C#/C++ only; PHP has a JavaScript base but no Dart one. The\n    52\t  JVM-only statements are helper-availability facts, not architecture.\n    53\t\n    54\t## The design in one paragraph\n    55\t\n    56\tANTLR already gives \"one grammar, N generated recognizers\". What the repo lacks\n    57\tis the other half: **one target-neutral parse-tree IR** that every host's\n    58\texporter produces and every language's CST-to-UAST converter consumes. That IR\n    59\texists today as CST packet v1; this plan names it, moves its ownership to the\n    60\tportable namespace, and makes the exporter a three-host namespace. A language\n    61\tthen is: a manifest (grammar + per-target helpers + patches + digests), a\n    62\t~40-line `parser.cljc` with three reader-conditional constructor profiles, the\n    63\tlanguage's own lowering over the IR, one catalog `install` line, and the\n    64\tconformance fixtures. Nothing per language touches the generator, the exporter,\n    65\tthe packet reader, or the REPL.\n    66\t\n    67\t## 1. The IR: `:yang.cst/v1`, formalized (owner addendum)\n    68\t\n    69\tRuling: **the IR is the existing CST packet shape, formalized, not a new\n    70\tschema.** Manifests already name it (`:yang.grammar/export-profile\n    71\t:yang.cst/v1`), `packet.cljc` already validates its tree structure, and every\n    72\tconsumer already reads it. A second schema would move every Python golden for\n    73\tno gain.\n    74\t\n    75\t- **Home**: `src/cljc/yang/antlr/packet.cljc` becomes the IR's owner. The shape\n    76\t  doc at `cst.clj:16-35` moves there verbatim as the normative statement (the\n    77\t  `cst` namespaces reference it, not the reverse). `packet-version` moves there\n    78\t  too. Nothing else in `packet.cljc` moves (D1, the diagnostic namespace, stays\n    79\t  with lift L2 of `yang.typescript.md` 17 to avoid two edits of one function).\n    80\t- **Normative invariants** (today implicit; written down so each target's\n    81\t  exporter can be checked against them):\n    82\t  1. ids are preorder indexes; `:root` is 0; child edges are a tree\n    83\t     (`validate!` enforces).\n    84\t  2. `:rule` is the grammar rule name from the parser's rule-name table, never\n    85\t     a target class name; `:type` is the symbolic token name, else the literal\n    86\t     name, else the decimal type, `\"EOF\"` for EOF.\n    87\t  3. `:span` is half-open **UTF-8 byte** offsets into the sealed source. Each\n    88\t     target's exporter converts its own index unit at the boundary (Java:\n    89\t     code points; JS and Dart: UTF-16 code units unless the stream is opened in\n    90\t     code-point mode; see risks).\n    91\t  4. A token is `:synthetic true` with a zero-width span when its text is not\n    92\t     the source at its span or it overlaps the previous real token.\n    93\t  5. A rule's span covers its real children only; an empty rule is zero-width\n    94\t     at its start token.\n    95\t  6. Any syntax error yields an error packet with `:yang.cst/errors` and no\n    96\t     nodes.\n    97\t  7. `:yang.cst/grammar` names the grammar revision and tool; the target does\n    98\t     not appear in the packet. Target-equivalence is the contract.\n    99\t- **Add** a portable `packet/conform` (name final; the engineer picks) that\n   100\t  checks invariants 2-5 over a packet against the source text: span\n   101\t  monotonicity in preorder, byte-span inside `[0, utf8-length]`, synthetic\n   102\t  tokens zero-width, rule spans covering children. It is the per-target\n   103\t  conformance oracle (section 5) and costs nothing at runtime (tests only).\n   104\t- **What the IR buys**: the UAST converter is written once per language against\n   105\t  data; a new target is an exporter change only; parity across hosts is\n   106\t  decidable by `=` on packets.\n   107\t\n   108\t## 2. The exporter: `yang.antlr.cst` on three hosts\n   109\t\n   110\tOne namespace, three host files, one contract. `cst.clj` stays as is;\n   111\t`src/cljs/yang/antlr/cst.cljs` and `src/cljd/yang/antlr/cst.cljd` implement the\n   112\tsame public names: `make-worker`, `parse-unit`, `parse-transform`,\n   113\t`utf8-bytes`. The profile contract is unchanged:\n   114\t`{:grammar-id :make-lexer :make-parser :start}`; only the constructor arguments\n   115\tare host objects.\n   116\t\n   117\t- **Node**: `antlr4` npm (`CharStreams.fromString` / `InputStream` with\n   118\t  `decodeToUnicodeCodePoints`), `CommonTokenStream`, a `ErrorListener`\n   119\t  subclass, `ctx.children`, `ctx.ruleIndex`, `parser.ruleNames`,\n   120\t  `parser.symbolicNames`/`literalNames`, `node.symbol.{type,start,stop,text}`.\n   121\t  `parse-transform` and `source-error` are host-free and should be shared: move\n   122\t  them to a cljc helper (`yang.antlr.cst.transform` or inside `packet.cljc`;\n   123\t  engineer's call, minimal diff) so each host file holds only the host walk.\n   124\t- **Dart**: pub `antlr4` (`InputStream.fromString`, `CommonTokenStream`,\n   125\t  `BaseErrorListener`, `ParserRuleContext.childCount/getChild`,\n   126\t  `TerminalNode.symbol`, `parser.ruleNames`, `parser.vocabulary`). Recorded\n   127\t  ClojureDart traps apply (reader-conditional ordering, `for` over chunked\n   128\t  seqs, multi-key `assoc` on nil, unary minus, protocol `[_ _]`).\n   129\t- **Worker isolation** (§4.3): the JVM builds fresh DFA arrays and prediction\n   130\t  caches per worker. Do the same where the runtime exposes\n   131\t  `LexerATNSimulator`/`ParserATNSimulator`/`PredictionContextCache` (both JS and\n   132\t  Dart do); if a host cannot, document that the host is single-threaded and the\n   133\t  shared cache is read-mostly, as §4.3 requires evidence not assertion.\n   134\t- **Probe** (`src/clj/yang/python/antlr/repl_probe.clj`): its lexical `scan`\n   135\t  and `probe` are pure; only `first-failure` touches the runtime. Split:\n   136\t  `scan`/`probe` to `src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/repl_probe.cljc`; the\n   137\t  host-specific `first-failure` becomes a `yang.antlr.cst` export\n   138\t  (`first-failure worker source` answering `nil | :end | :early`) implemented\n   139\t  per host. The probe then needs no reader conditional and a new language's\n   140\t  probe reuses the same primitive.\n   141\t\n   142\t## 3. The build: one manifest, N targets\n   143\t\n   144\t`antlr/<grammar>/manifest.edn` grows a `:targets` map; the generator\n   145\t(`yang_antlr_gen.clj`) loops over it. Java stays byte-identical (its generated\n   146\tdigests must not move).\n   147\t\n   148\t```clojure\n   149\t{:grammar {... :files {... \"JavaScript/Python3LexerBase.js\" {:blake3 \"..\"}\n   150\t                       \"JavaScript/Python3ParserBase.js\" {:blake3 \"..\"}\n   151\t                       \"Dart/Python3LexerBase.dart\" {:blake3 \"..\"}\n   152\t                       \"Dart/Python3ParserBase.dart\" {:blake3 \"..\"}}}\n   153\t :tool {:antlr \"4.13.2\"}\n   154\t :targets\n   155\t {:java       {:runtime \"org.antlr/antlr4-runtime 4.13.2\"\n   156\t               :flags [\"-Dlanguage=Java\" \"-package\" \"yang.python.antlr.gen\" \"-no-listener\" \"-no-visitor\"]\n   157\t               :helpers [\"Java/Python3LexerBase.java\" \"Java/Python3ParserBase.java\"]\n   158\t               :package \"yang.python.antlr.gen\"      ; the package-clause prepend stays Java-only\n   159\t               :out \"build/antlr/python3/java\"       ; then javac -> build/antlr/python3/classes\n   160\t               :patches {...}                        ; the existing four\n   161\t               :generated {...}}\n   162\t  :javascript {:runtime \"antlr4 4.13.2 (npm)\"\n   163\t               :flags [\"-Dlanguage=JavaScript\" \"-no-listener\" \"-no-visitor\"]\n   164\t               :helpers [\"JavaScript/Python3LexerBase.js\" \"JavaScript/Python3ParserBase.js\"]\n   165\t               :out \"build/antlr/python3/js/yang/antlr/gen/python3\"\n   166\t               :patches {\"JavaScript/Python3LexerBase.js\" [...]}   ; the ported positioned-token patch\n   167\t               :generated {...}}\n   168\t  :dart       {:runtime \"antlr4 4.13.2 (pub)\"\n   169\t               :flags [\"-Dlanguage=Dart\" \"-no-listener\" \"-no-visitor\"]\n   170\t               :helpers [\"Dart/Python3LexerBase.dart\" \"Dart/Python3ParserBase.dart\"]\n   171\t               :out \"lib/antlr/python3\"\n   172\t               :patches {\"Dart/Python3LexerBase.dart\" [...]}\n   173\t               :generated {...}}}\n   174\t :license {...}            ; four more \"no notice; UNRESOLVED\" rows\n   175\t :redistribution {...}}    ; unchanged policy, extended to the new helpers\n   176\t```\n   177\t\n   178\t- The ANTLR tool is already on the `:antlr-gen` classpath; `-Dlanguage=` is the\n   179\t  only per-target switch. Helper files are fetched by digest into\n   180\t  `build/antlr/<grammar>/upstream` exactly as today (owner decision \"vendor .g4\n   181\t  only\" stands; the earlier `5c-antlr-build.log` failure was a network-less\n   182\t  first fetch and is unchanged by this plan).\n   183\t- **Patches per target**: the Java `:patches` entries become the pattern; the\n   184\t  JS and Dart ports replace `commonToken(NEWLINE, text)`/`DEDENT` construction\n   185\t  with positioned tokens (`CommonToken` with explicit start/stop in both\n   186\t  runtimes). Exact-text, applied once, digest-pinned, as today.\n   187\t- **Digests**: `:generated` per target; `generated-digests.edn` per target\n   188\t  under the target's out dir. ASCII check per target (§4.5).\n   189\t- **Where generated code lands and how hosts load it**:\n   190\t  - Java: unchanged (`deps.edn :paths` has `build/antlr/python3/classes`).\n   191\t  - Node: `build/antlr/python3/js` is added to the `:cljs` alias\n   192\t    `:extra-paths`; the generated ESM files sit under\n   193\t    `yang/antlr/gen/python3/` so cljs requires them as classpath JS:\n   194\t    `(:require [\"/yang/antlr/gen/python3/Python3Parser.js\" :as Python3Parser])`\n   195\t    and the runtime as `[\"antlr4\" :as antlr4]`. `package.json` gains\n   196\t    `\"antlr4\": \"4.13.2\"` (exact pin, as `@noble/hashes` is pinned).\n   197\t  - Dart: `lib/` is gitignored and is the Dart package root\n   198\t    (`datomworld_cljd`), so generated files go to `lib/antlr/python3/` and\n   199\t    cljd requires `[\"package:datomworld_cljd/antlr/python3/Python3Parser.dart\"\n   200\t    :as p3]`. `pubspec.yaml` gains `antlr4: 4.13.2`.\n   201\t- **bb.edn**: `gen:python-antlr` becomes `gen:antlr` (all grammars, all\n   202\t  targets, `--grammar`/`--target` filters); `test:cljs` and `test:cljd` gain\n   203\t  `:depends [gen:antlr]` like `test:clj` has today. No compat alias (dev-only\n   204\t  repo).\n   205\t- **Versioning**: tool and three runtimes are one release; a bump regenerates\n   206\t  all targets and re-pins all digests in one commit (§4.2).\n   207\t\n   208\t## 4. The parser namespace per language\n   209\t\n   210\t`src/clj/yang/python/antlr/parser.clj` becomes\n   211\t`src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/parser.cljc`:\n   212\t\n   213\t```clojure\n   214\t(ns yang.python.antlr.parser\n   215\t  (:require [yang.antlr.cst :as cst] [yang.stage :as stage]\n   216\t            #?@(:clj [] :cljs [[\"antlr4\" :as antlr4]\n   217\t                               [\"/yang/antlr/gen/python3/Python3Lexer.js\" :default Python3Lexer] ...]\n   218\t                :cljd [[\"package:datomworld_cljd/antlr/python3/Python3Lexer.dart\" :as l] ...]))\n   219\t  #?(:clj (:import (yang.python.antlr.gen Python3Lexer Python3Parser))))\n   220\t\n   221\t(def profile\n   222\t  {:grammar-id grammar-id\n   223\t   :make-lexer  #?(:clj (fn [cs] (Python3Lexer. cs)) :cljs (fn [cs] (Python3Lexer. cs)) :cljd (fn [cs] (l/Python3Lexer cs)))\n   224\t   :make-parser ...\n   225\t   :start       #?(:clj (fn [p] (.file_input p)) :cljs (fn [p] (.file_input p)) :cljd (fn [p] (.file_input p)))})\n   226\t;; make-worker, parse-source, open-stage, step-stage unchanged\n   227\t```\n   228\t\n   229\t`#?(:cljd ...)` must come first in each conditional (recorded trap). This file\n   230\tis the entire per-language, per-host surface.\n   231\t\n   232\t## 5. CST equivalence: the parity story\n   233\t\n   234\tThree layers, all reusing existing tests by widening their host set:\n   235\t\n   236\t1. **Exporter conformance (per target, language-free)**: a new cljc\n   237\t   `yang.antlr.conformance` test helper takes a profile and a fixture list\n   238\t   `[source expected-shape]` and asserts, on the running host: packet\n   239\t   `validate!` and `conform` pass; the `shape` (rule/type/text/span) equals the\n   240\t   golden; spans are UTF-8 bytes (`\"s = 'é𝄞'\\nt = 2\\n\"` → `[4 12]`,\n   241\t   `[13 14]`, root end = `utf8-bytes`); syntax errors yield error packets with\n   242\t   no nodes; EOF/DEDENT synthetic and zero-width. `cst_export_test.clj` becomes\n   243\t   `.cljc` and runs on all three hosts through it. The goldens are the JVM's\n   244\t   today; they become the cross-target oracle.\n   245\t2. **Packet parity (per language)**: `precompiled-packets-are-the-parsers-test`\n   246\t   in `test/yin/repl/python_antlr_test.cljc` loses its `#?(:cljd nil :clj ...)`\n   247\t   wrap: on every host the installed parser's packets equal the precompiled\n   248\t   ones. `langs` becomes `[:yang.python/cst :yang.python/antlr]` on all hosts.\n   249\t3. **Program parity (end to end)**: the REPL session scenarios of that test\n   250\t   plus the prompt-suppress regression and the owner's comprehension line run\n   251\t   through `:yang.python/antlr` on Node and Dart; expected output is the JVM's.\n   252\t   Because the lowering is already portable and already proven on hand-built\n   253\t   packets, parity at layer 2 implies parity at layer 3; layer 3 is the\n   254\t   acceptance the owner sees at the prompt.\n   255\t\n   256\tOptional later corpus: grammars-v4's `python/python3/small` inputs as a\n   257\tthree-host packet-equality corpus (slow tier).\n   258\t\n   259\t## 6. Catalog and SPI changes\n   260\t\n   261\t- `frontends.cljc`: remove the `#?(:cljd [] :clj ...)` wrappers around the\n   262\t  requires, `antlr-manifest` and the install; the ANTLR frontend installs on\n   263\t  every host. The manifest bumps `:yang.frontend/revision` (\"f3\" → the next)\n   264\t  and its grammar map gains `:yang.grammar/targets #{:java :javascript :dart}`\n   265\t  (portable data, informational; `select` can pin on it). No SPI revision bump:\n   266\t  the key is optional and `yang.frontend/grammar-checks` is unchanged.\n   267\t- \"Installed where its parser runs\" docstrings flip. `unavailable-parser`\n   268\t  remains the answer only when a host has no target generated for that grammar\n   269\t  (future languages, section 7).\n   270\t- `yang.antlr.md` amendments (dated): 4.1 table (Dart row: generated Dart\n   271\t  parser is now the local implementation for Python), 8.5.6 F2/F3 acceptance\n   272\t  lines (\"since the parser is JVM-only\" struck; replaced by the parity layers),\n   273\t  5.5 points to `packet.cljc` as the IR owner; `yin-repl-design.md` Python\n   274\t  section; `yang.typescript.md` 2.2 and 5.3 and `yang.php.md` 6: the sentence\n   275\t  \"the ANTLR parser is JVM-only for both\" becomes \"per-target helper\n   276\t  availability decides the host matrix (yang.antlr.md 4.2); TS has Java-only\n   277\t  helpers upstream, PHP Java and JavaScript\".\n   278\t\n   279\t## 7. Adding a language after this lands (the mechanical recipe)\n   280\t\n   281\tFor language L with grammars-v4 grammar G:\n   282\t\n   283\t1. `antlr/<G>/manifest.edn`: vendor the `.g4` files, list helper files per\n   284\t   target that upstream ships (or that you port), patches, licence rows. If a\n   285\t   target has no helper (and the grammar needs one), omit that target: that host\n   286\t   answers `unavailable-parser` for L until a helper is ported. This is the only\n   287\t   non-mechanical step and it is bounded to the helper file.\n   288\t2. `bb gen:antlr --grammar <G>`; record the printed digests into `:generated`.\n   289\t3. `src/cljc/yang/<L>/antlr/parser.cljc`: the profile with three constructor\n   290\t   conditionals (section 4). About 40 lines.\n   291\t4. `src/cljc/yang/<L>/antlr/lower.cljc` and friends: the language's CST-to-UAST\n   292\t   converter over the IR through `packet/*` only (section 8 boundary).\n   293\t5. Conformance fixtures: `[source shape]` goldens for `yang.antlr.conformance`;\n   294\t   precompiled packets for the parity test.\n   295\t6. `frontends.cljc`: one `install` line with the manifest.\n   296\t7. Optional: a probe (`scan` in cljc over `cst/first-failure`).\n   297\t\n   298\tSteps 2, 3, 5, 6 are the same for every language and every target; the\n   299\tgenerator, exporter, packet reader, REPL and catalog are not edited. TypeScript\n   300\tand PHP (`yang.typescript.md` 5, `yang.php.md` 6) already follow steps 1 and 3\n   301\tin their designs; they gain Node/Dart the moment a helper exists for the\n   302\ttarget.\n   303\t\n   304\t## 8. Boundary: what stays per language\n   305\t\n   306\t- Per language, never generic: the lowering (`lower.cljc`), scope analysis,\n   307\t  prelude, publication, primitives, the probe's lexical `scan` rules, the\n   308\t  refusal and diagnostic namespaces, entry-rule choice (`file_input`), the\n   309\t  trailing-newline normalization in the REPL `:parse` binding.\n   310\t- Generic, never language-aware: the generator, `packet.cljc`, the three\n   311\t  `yang.antlr.cst` files, `yang.antlr.conformance`, `yang.frontend`.\n   312\t- The IR carries rule and token **names**; no language's exporter may add\n   313\t  language-specific fields to packets. A language that needs more than the IR\n   314\t  provides (for example alternative labels, §5.5) proposes an IR version bump,\n   315\t  not a private field.\n   316\t\n   317\t## 9. Reconciliation with the TS and PHP designs\n   318\t\n   319\t- **(a) Already covered**: the manifest shape and vendoring discipline\n   320\t  (`yang.typescript.md` 5.1, `yang.php.md` 6), the worker profile contract\n   321\t  (`cst/make-worker`, \"nothing in cst.clj changes\"), the per-target helper\n   322\t  versioning rule (`yang.antlr.md` 4.2), the lifts L1-L4 (section 17) that make\n   323\t  the lowering side shared. This plan edits none of them.\n   324\t- **(b) Genuinely new for Python on JS/Dart**: multi-target generation with\n   325\t  per-target patches and digests; the Node and Dart exporters; the IR\n   326\t  formalization and `conform`; the conformance helper; the probe split; the\n   327\t  catalog gate removal; the dependency pins.\n   328\t- **(c) Generalizes**: everything in (b) except the Python manifest rows and\n   329\t  the Python parser profile. TS and PHP become \"add the JS/Dart helper to the\n   330\t  manifest\" problems.\n   331\t- **Collision to sequence**: L2 edits `packet.cljc` (D1). This plan edits\n   332\t  `packet.cljc` too (the doc move and `conform`). Whichever lands second\n   333\t  rebases; the edits touch different functions.\n   334\t\n   335\t## 10. Legacy retirement (separate round, gated)\n   336\t\n   337\tPreconditions, all demonstrable before the round opens: M2 landed (section\n   338\t11) with layers 1-3 green on three hosts; the owner's comprehension line\n   339\tevaluates on Node and Dart; every `yang.python` (legacy) test scenario is\n   340\teither ported to the ANTLR frontend or recorded as intentionally dropped; the\n   341\t`yang.python.md`-era docs name the retirement. The round then deletes\n   342\t`src/cljc/yang/python.cljc` and its tests, the `:yang.python/legacy` install,\n   343\tand amends the docs. Not part of the first engineer round.\n   344\t\n   345\t## 11. Implementation slices (first engineer round builds M0, M1, M2 in order)\n   346\t\n   347\t| Slice | Scope | Acceptance |\n   348\t|---|---|---|\n   349\t| M0 build | manifest `:targets`, generator loop, JS and Dart generation with ported positioned-token patches, digests, `gen:antlr`, `package.json`/`pubspec.yaml` pins, `:cljs` extra-path, lane `:depends` | Java `:generated` digests byte-identical to today; JS and Dart artifacts generated, ASCII, digest-pinned; `bb gen:antlr` reproducible from clean; `npm ci` and `pub get` resolve 4.13.2 |\n   350\t| M1 IR + exporters | `packet.cljc` owns the v1 shape doc and `conform`; `cst.cljs`, `cst.cljd`; shared `parse-transform`; `yang.antlr.conformance`; `cst_export_test` → cljc | conformance goldens green on JVM, Node, Dart including the UTF-8 span fixture and error packets; `conform` passes on every golden and on every precompiled packet |\n   351\t| M2 Python on three hosts | `parser.cljc`; probe split; `frontends.cljc` gate removed, revision bump, `:yang.grammar/targets`; `python_antlr_test` `langs` and parity test on all hosts; e2e and comprehension line on Node and Dart; doc amendments of section 6 | layers 2 and 3 green on three hosts; `(lang :yang.python/antlr)` at the Node and Dart prompts evaluates the comprehension line; quick tiers green per host; kondo + cljstyle clean |\n   352\t| M3 legacy retirement | section 10 | its own round |\n   353\t\n   354\tLanes per slice: `bb test:clj` per iteration; full three-host quick tier at\n   355\teach landing, one lane set at a time, foreground; focused ANTLR namespaces\n   356\twith `DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1` at M2.\n   357\t\n   358\t## 12. Risks\n   359\t\n   360\t- **Index units**: JS and Dart streams index UTF-16 code units by default; the\n   361\t  JVM indexes code points. The exporter must open code-point streams or map\n   362\t  units; the `é𝄞` fixture is the guard. Highest-probability parity defect.\n   363\t- **Dart target untested upstream**: `desc.xml` excludes Dart; the Dart helper\n   364\t  may lag the grammar (method names, token constants `TOKEN_NEWLINE`). Bounded:\n   365\t  it is one file under patch control. Also the pub runtime's SDK bound\n   366\t  `<3.0.0` (Dart 3 accepts null-safe `<3.0.0` packages, but verify `pub get`\n   367\t  under the project's `>=3.7.0`).\n   368\t- **Circular ESM import** between the JS lexer base and the generated parser;\n   369\t  shadow-cljs classpath-JS handling of ESM plus an npm import must be verified\n   370\t  in M0 (fallback: `:js-options :resolve` to the build dir).\n   371\t- **Licence**: four more helpers without notices; `:redistribution :blocked-until`\n   372\t  stays. Runtimes are BSD-3-Clause.\n   373\t- **Bundle/CI cost**: `antlr4` npm (~0.3 MB) enters the `yin-repl` node-script\n   374\t  and the test build only, not the browser `:demo` build unless required; the\n   375\t  Dart lane recompiles the generated parser (large) per `flutter test` shard.\n   376\t  Measure in M2; acceptable for a dev-only repo.\n   377\t- **Generation needs network on first run** (helper fetch), as today; four\n   378\t  more files.\n   379\t- **Worker isolation evidence** on single-threaded hosts (section 2).\n   380\t- **Chunked-seq and reader-conditional traps** on Dart in the exporter walk.\n   381\t\n   382\t## Verification (end to end)\n   383\t\n   384\t1. `bb gen:antlr` from a clean `build/` and `lib/antlr`; compare Java digests\n   385\t   with `git show HEAD:antlr/python3/manifest.edn`.\n   386\t2. `bb test:clj`, `bb test:cljs`, `bb test:cljd` quick tiers, foreground, one at\n   387\t   a time; confirm `Testing yang.antlr.conformance-test` and\n   388\t   `yin.repl.python-antlr-test` appear in the Node and Dart output.\n   389\t3. At the Node prompt (`node target/yin-repl.js`) and the Dart peer:\n   390\t   `(lang :yang.python/antlr)` then the owner's comprehension line; output\n   391\t   equals the JVM's.\n   392\t4. `DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1` focused ANTLR namespaces.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
